        <h2 class="subhead" id="mini-regex">Overview</h1>
            <p>
                <!--This project was done as part of ECE354? for planning and implementing some sort of HW accelerated task with a Nexys Video FPGA.

                The main goal of this project was to implement HW acceleration of chracter regcognition. The original goal was to use
                a external camera to be able to classify handwritten text but we pivoted to HDMI after some difficulties getting the camera to work.



                Although the classfying algorithim was not very accurate or usefull, this system shows a proof of concept for HW accelerated text recognition.

                In the future, the K-means classifier could be replaced by more accurate algorithim. And there might be an automated algorithim to
                decided what part of the sub-image we care about. and maybe some more logic to do somthing with the generated text result.

                Right now this project is just interesting in the  methods used to develop the HW IP, and how Vivado IP was integrated to make a functioning system.

                I will mainly go over the portions of this project I worked on and think are the most interesting (box reszing)-->

            </p>
            <p>
                In this project, a nexys video FPGA was used to implment the system shown below. The FPGA intercepts the HDMI signal comming from
                a latop, or other HDMI source, crops the image based on user inputs, then tries to classify the result as a number. <br>
                
                Although this is not a particularily usfull use case, this project is interesting as it deals with working with 
                a high speed video stream and provides low latency, high frame rate classification, and the resizing logic was non trivial to implement.
                


            </p>
            <p>
               <p><img src="/imgs/classification_all.png" alt="img" width="50%" height="auto"></p>
            </p>
            <p>
                The input HDMI video stream is resized to a 28x28 video stream based on the sub-box slection of the user using the resizing IP.
            </p>
            <p>
                Every input frame is stored in a SRAM buffer and classified using the K-means algorithim.
            </p>
            <p>
                A microblaze controller is run in parallel with this process which captures the output result (i.e. a number 1-9), and the 28x28 frame from the SRAM buffer,
                and then displays this info on the OLED screen, operating at the same frame rate as the HDMI input.
            </p>

